apple-dabinett,apple,fruit-apple,Dabinett,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"Thought to have originated in the Martock-Kingsbury area of Somerset in the mid-19th Century. Believed to have been named after a Mr Dabinett. Possibly a seedling of Chisel Jersey. This variety crops regularly. Fruits have slightly crisp flesh with a sweet, astringent, strong fruity flavour when ripe. Dabinett is weak grower producing a small and spreading tree. A bittersweet variety that produces a soft, full-bodied, high quality cider.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 077 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Dabinett,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=1438&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),1438,,05-14,05-21,,Mid November,21,,,,,,,,,,,,small-medium,Globose,weak-medium,weak,Green yellow,high-very high,Red,striped/solid flush,low,,Cream,50.00 mm,62.40 mm,,,
apple-yarlington-mill,apple,fruit-apple,Yarlington Mill,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"Originated in the village of yarlington, in the North Cadbury area of Somerset. A strongly biennial cropper unless pruned regularly. Produces a medium bittersweet cider.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 119 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Yarlington Mill,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=6962&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),6962,,05-04,05-12,,Late October,19,,,,,,,,,,,,small-medium,Globose conical,weak-medium,weak,Green yellow,high,Red,striped/solid flush,low,,Cream,54.70 mm,63.60 mm,,,
apple-kingston-black,apple,fruit-apple,Kingston Black,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"Believed to be a Somerset apple and possibly raised at Kingston, near Taunton. This variety was introduced into Herefordshire c.1820 by Mr Palmer of Bollitree Estate, Weston-under-Penyard near Ross-on-Wye. Trees are of medium size and have a spreading habit. Susceptible to scab. Fruits are medium to small in size and produce a full bodied, excellent quality cider with a distinctive flavour.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1999 - 096 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Kingston Black,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=3250&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),3250,,05-14,05-22,,late september,21,,,,,,,,,,,,small,Globose,medium,very weak,Green yellow,,Red,striped/solid flush,low,,,45.75 mm,52.85 mm,,,
apple-michelin,apple,fruit-apple,Michelin,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"This old popular cider apple was raised by M. Legrand of Yvetot, Normandy , France. It first fruited in 1872. It was named after M. Michelin of Paris, one of the original promoters appointed by the French Government for the study of cider fruits. Introduced into Herefordshire in 1884 by the Woolhope Naturalists Field Club. Fruits are small and pale green to yellow. Produces a sweet juice resulting in a bittersweet cider ideal for blending.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 096 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Michelin,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=3956&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),3956,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,small,Globose conical,weak-medium,weak,Green yellow,,Orange,washed out,low,,,43.00 mm,47.60 mm,,,
apple-brown-snout,apple,fruit-apple,Brown Snout,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"Thought to have originated in Herefordshire, UK. Known to have been in existence in the mid 1800s. Late flowering. Moderately biennial. Fruits are small to medium with firm flesh. Produces a bittersweet, average mild to medium cider.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 068 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Brown Snout,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=790&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),790,,05-24,05-30,,Early November,22,,,,,,,,,,,,small,Globose conical,weak-medium,absent,Whitish green,high,Red,striped/mottled,low,,White,39.70 mm,45.35 mm,,,
apple-sweet-coppin,apple,fruit-apple,Sweet Coppin,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"An old variety originating in Devon. Once very common in cider orchards in the Exeter area. Susceptible to mildew. The medium to large fruits ripen early November. Produces a pure sweet, sometimes a very mild bittersweet cider of good quality.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 112 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Sweet Coppin,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=6196&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),6196,,05-10,05-17,,Early November,20,,,,,,,,,,,,small,Globose conical,weak,very weak,Green yellow,very low,Pink,mottled,medium-high,,White,49.10 mm,52.80 mm,,,
apple-chisel-jersey,apple,fruit-apple,Chisel Jersey,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,An old cider variety known to have been widely grown around the Martock area of Somerset. The name is thought to have derived from the term,,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 072 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Chisel Jersey,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=1094&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),1094,,05-18,05-25,,Mid November,21,,,,,,,,,,,,small-medium,Broad globose conical,weak-medium,weak,Green yellow,high,Orange,striped/solid flush,low,,Cream,45.65 mm,58.40 mm,,,
apple-harry-masters-jersey,apple,fruit-apple,Harry Master's Jersey,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,Also known by the name,,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 089 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Harry Masters Jersey,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=2636&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),2636,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,medium,Globose conical,medium,weak-medium,Green yellow,,Red,striped/washed out,low,,,53.60 mm,62.70 mm,,,
apple-somerset-redstreak,apple,fruit-apple,Somerset Redstreak,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"Thought to have originated in the Sutton Montis area of Somerset. As a result of its good performance in a 1917 trial at the National Fruit & Cider Institute together with good orchard performance at Burghill, Hereford, it was subsequently propagated to be included in many commercial cider orchards. The medium sized fruit ripens in October. Produces a mild or medium bittersweet cider of average quality.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 106 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Somerset Redstreak,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=5889&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),5889,,05-08,05-16,,Mid October,20,,,,,,,,,,,,medium,Globose conical,medium-strong,very weak,Whitish yellow,medium-high,Red,striped/solid flush,low,,Cream,54.40 mm,67.00 mm,,,
apple-stoke-red,apple,fruit-apple,Stoke Red,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"This variety gained attention in the 1920s when surveys found trees growing in Rodney Stoke, Somerset. The trees are fairly vigorous and crop quite heavily. Produces a sharp, slightly astringent juice and a fine, sharp cider.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 110 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Stoke Red,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=6039&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),6039,,05-18,05-27,,Mid November,21,,,,,,,,,,,,small,Broad globose conical,weak,weak,Green yellow,high,Orange,striped/solid flush,low,,Cream,39.78 mm,49.78 mm,,,
apple-broxwood-foxwhelp,apple,fruit-apple,Broxwood Foxwhelp,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"Known to have been planted in the orchard of H.P. Bulmer & Co. Ltd., in the 1920s. Thought to be a sport of Foxwhelp. The small fruits produce a medium bittersharp cider and a full-bodied juice.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 124 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Broxwood Foxwhelp,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=800&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),800,,05-01,05-08,,Mid September,19,,,,,,,,,,,,small,Globose conical,weak-medium,very weak,Whitish yellow,high-very high,Red,striped/solid flush,low,,Cream,47.20 mm,53.80 mm,,,
apple-tremletts-bitter,apple,fruit-apple,Tremlett's Bitter,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"Originated in the Exe Valley, Devon. Flowers are very sensitive to frost which may contribute to the trees biennial cropping pattern. Susceptible to scab. Produces a full bittersweet cider.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 117 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Tremlett's Bitter,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=6409&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),6409,,04-29,05-08,,Early October,19,,,,,,,,,,,,small-medium,Globose conical,medium,weak,Green yellow,high,Orange,striped/mottled,low,,White,57.80 mm,60.70 mm,,,
apple-bulmers-norman,apple,fruit-apple,Bulmer's Norman,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"Originally an unnamed variety imported from Normandy, France. It was developed by H.P. Bulmer & Co., Ltd., in Hereford, England. Fruits are medium to large. Produces a good yield but tends to be biennial. The flesh is white with a woolly texture and a sweet but astringent flavour. Triploid. Trees are very vigorous and with a spreading habit and branch breakage can occur when the crop is heavy. Susceptible to scab. Fruits produce a bittersweet, fast-fermenting medium cider.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 070 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Bulmer's Norman,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=826&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),826,,05-04,05-11,,Mid October,19,,,,true,,,,,,,,small-medium,Globose conical,strong,weak-medium,Whitish yellow,absent,n/a,n/a,low,,White,44.07 mm,57.13 mm,,,
apple-major,apple,fruit-apple,Major,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,Originated in South Devon. Once a commonly grown variety in some of the old farm orchards of Devon and Somerset. Trees are quite vigorous. Fruits are of medium size. Produces a good bittersweet cider.,,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 125 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Major,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=3745&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),3745,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,small,Globose conical,medium,medium,Yellow,,Orange,striped/solid flush,low,,,56.80 mm,58.20 mm,,,
apple-ellis-bitter,apple,fruit-apple,Ellis Bitter,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"An old variety thought to have originated on the farm of a Mr Ellis of Newton St Cyres, South Devon. The large, conical fruits have white, crisp, juicy sweet flesh which is a little astringent. A medium bittersweet variety which produces a good quality cider.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 083 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Ellis Bitter,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=1835&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),1835,,05-11,05-20,,Mid October,20,,,,,,,,,,,,medium-large,Conical,medium,weak,Yellow,high-very high,Red,striped/solid flush,low,,Cream,65.90 mm,72.80 mm,firm-very firm,,
apple-porters-perfection,apple,fruit-apple,Porter's Perfection,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"Originated in the orchard of Charles Porter of East Lambrook, Somerset in the 19th Century. Introduced in 1907. Produces a sharp juice with little astringency. Ripens late October to early November.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 102 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Porter's Perfection,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=4777&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),4777,,05-04,05-12,,Late October,19,,,,,,,,,,,,small,Broad globose conical,weak-medium,weak,Green yellow,high,Red,striped/solid flush,low,,Cream,37.90 mm,44.50 mm,,,
apple-court-royal,apple,fruit-apple,Court Royal,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"Origin unknown but once grown in Somerset, East Devon and Herefordshire. In the early 1900s it was used as a dessert variety. Fruits are slightly crisp and sweet. A triploid. Tree growth is vigorous producing a large tree. Produces a sweet fast fermenting juice.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 075 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Court Royal,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=1312&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),1312,,04-30,05-08,,Mid October,19,,,,true,,,,,,,,medium,Flat,weak-medium,very weak,Whitish green,low-medium,Orange,striped/mottled,low,,Cream,52.00 mm,69.70 mm,,,
apple-browns-apple,apple,fruit-apple,Brown's Apple,Malus domestica Borkh.,,"Originated in Devon. It was discovered by Mr Hill, a cider maker and nurseryman of Staverton, near Totnes, Devon. Known to have been in existence in the early 1920s. Trees are very vigourous and can therefore delay cropping. Fruits are medium sized with white flesh which often carries a red tinge especially in highly coloured fruits. Produces a medium-sharp, fruity but rather thin cider.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 067 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Brown's Apple,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=1595&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),1595,,05-04,05-14,,Late October,20,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
apple-ashton-bitter,apple,fruit-apple,Ashton Bitter,Malus domestica Borkh.,Cider,"Raised by Mr G.T. Spinks, Long Ashton Research Station, Bristol in 1947. A seedling raised from a Dabinett/Stoke Red cross. A precocious, midseason flowering variety. Fruits are of medium size and firm. Produces juice with low acidity and medium tannin.",,Material available from the National Fruit Collection. Material held in Tidnor Wood National Collection® of Malus (Cider making) Accession No. 1989 - 129 fingerprint check_circle Accession name Ashton Bitter,True,,https://www.nationalfruitcollection.org.uk/full2.php?id=233&&fruit=apple,Open Government Licence (Crown Copyright; excluding logos),233,,05-18,05-27,,Late September,21,,,,,,,,,,,,small,Globose conical,weak,weak,Whitish yellow,medium-high,Red,striped/washed out,low,,Cream,46.26 mm,51.68 mm,soft,,
